  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nHigh pressure slides off the East Coast through tonight. Subtle\nreturn flow will kick in later this afternoon, with lighter \nsoutherly flow continuing through the night. This should lead to\na warmer night than last night, with a cast of cirrus \npreventing the good radiational cooling setup we had last night.\n\nRadar to the west does depict some rain ahead of a lifting warm\nfront, and wouldn't count out a few showers or sprinkles making\nit to the mountains and foothills tonight. But this will need \nto overcome a large amount of dry air, so kept these chances \nfocused after midnight. \n\nWednesday will bring warmer temperatures despite increased \ncloudiness. This will arrive ahead of an incoming cold front. \nThere will be an increase in moisture, but forcing is weak. NBM \nkeeps no mention of showers in the forecast area, but don't \nthink a brief shower can be completely ruled out midday. Timing\nlow and upper moisture would be key, and right now this is a \nnarrow window before deeper moisture pushes east by late \nafternoon.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nWe will end the work week under near zonal flow aloft with a fast \njet to our north and a strong ridge to our south. At the surface, \nhigh pressure will move out into the Atlantic on Thursday as a weak \ncold front approaches from the west. The southern ridge will also \nretrograde a little bit, transitioning our region into some broad \ncyclonic flow aloft. All of these factors will lead to increasing \nprecipitation chances (30 to 60 percent) Thursday afternoon and \nnight. Most of the rain will move out on early Friday morning, but \nwe could see a few showers lingering across the north. As far as \ntotals go, this system still appears to be fairly low impact with \nNBM probabilities only maxing out around 50 percent for a quarter \ninch or more. As of now, it appears the best chances will develop \nover portions of southern and central New Hampshire before moving \ninto Interior and Coastal Maine. \n\nFriday and Saturday should be mainly dry as high pressure moves back \ninto the region. Most ensembles then have a shortwave or two moving \nthrough Sunday or at the beginning of the next work week, which will \nlead to our next precipitation chances. For several days, this next \nsystem appeared to be more favorable for some widespread rain but \nthe 12z model runs have trended a bit further south. Timing also WPC \ncontinues to be very uncertain as several ensemble members bring \nchances in as early as Sunday afternoon and some do not bring rain \nin until Monday or Tuesday. Some members keep precipitation almost \ncompletely out of forecast area. Thus, there are still many \npotential solutions on the table and we will hopefully see some \nbetter model agreement in the coming days. \n\nRegarding temperatures, highs will mainly be in the mid 60s to mid \n70s Thursday and Friday (may a few upper 70s over southern New \nHampshire). We then cool down over the weekend and into next week \nwith highs mainly in the 60s.",
